<article class="ltx_document ltx_authors_1line" lang="en-US">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Jörg Martin
</span><span id="id1" class="ltx_note ltx_note_frontmatter ltx_thanks_correspondence ltx_role_thanks"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">†</sup><span class="ltx_note_type">thanks: </span>Corresponding author: joerg.martin@ptb.de</span></span></span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt, Berlin, Germany
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Stefan Haufe
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Physikalisch-Technische Bundesanstalt, Berlin, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Technische Universität Berlin, Berlin, Germany
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Charité – Universitätsmedizin Berlin, Berlin, Germany
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Explainable artificial intelligence promises to yield insights into relevant features, thereby enabling humans to examine and scrutinize machine learning models or even facilitating scientific discovery. Considering the widespread technique of Shapley values, we find that purely data-driven operationalization of multivariate feature importance is unsuitable for such purposes. Even for simple problems with two features, spurious associations due to collider bias and suppression arise from considering one feature only in the observational context of the other, which can lead to misinterpretations. Causal knowledge about the data-generating process is required to identify and correct such misleading feature attributions. We propose cc-Shapley (causal context Shapley), an interventional modification of conventional observational Shapley values leveraging knowledge of the data’s causal structure, thereby analyzing the relevance of a feature in the causal context of the remaining features. We show theoretically that this eradicates spurious association induced by collider bias. We compare the behavior of Shapley and cc-Shapley values on various, synthetic, and real-world datasets. We observe nullification or reversal of associations compared to univariate feature importance when moving from observational to cc-Shapley.</p>
  
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Feature attribution is a common paradigm in the field of Explainable Artificial Intelligence (XAI).
Its goal is to quantify the importance of each feature by attributing a score to it.
It is often assumed that using such techniques allows us to check whether a certain model is “correct”, i.e., whether it misbehaves on certain data points or whether it uses unexpected, possibly unfavorable, structures for its prediction <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ribeiro et al., 2016</a>, <a href="#bib.bib36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lapuschkin et al., 2019</a>, <a href="#bib.bib37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Anders et al., 2022</a>]</cite>. Likewise, XAI is hoped to boost scientific discovery by unveiling hidden multivariate patterns in the data that are associated with prediction targets of interest <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Jiménez-Luna et al., 2020</a>, <a href="#bib.bib40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wong et al., 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Samek and Müller, 2019</a>]</cite>. All of these promises are based on the assumption that the attributions gained with such methods do not show spurious patterns and can be interpreted unambiguously. This work aims to show that this assumption is an illusion unless we include causal knowledge into our considerations.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Our analysis focuses on Shapley values, a concept of game-theoretical origin that has become a well-established approach to XAI. When predicting a target variable <math id="S1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> from features <math id="S1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Shapley value</em> of a feature <math id="S1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> is defined as<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
            <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
            
            
            
          There are various versions of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>), cf. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.</span></span></span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shapley and others, 1953</a>]</cite></p>
<table id="S1.EGx1" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi(X_{j})=\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}{\small\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}}I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒮</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒮</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi(X_{j})=\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}{\small\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}}I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(1)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">where we introduce the notation</p>
<table id="S1.EGx2" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(2)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.p2.3" class="ltx_p">for the change in prediction when we add observation <math id="S1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to the observed features <math id="S1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>.
Formula (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) computes the weighted sum of these changes, where the combinatorial weights can be interpreted through the probability of randomly drawing <math id="S1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>. For binary <math id="S1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\{0,1\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\{0,1\}</annotation></semantics></math>, as considered below, (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>) simplifies to a difference of probabilities.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The computational complexity behind (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) quickly increases with <math id="S1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}|</annotation></semantics></math>. For this reason, several scalable modifications have been proposed, e.g., by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee [2017]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janzing et al. [2020]</a></cite>. We show, however, that even without any approximations put on top, <math id="S1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> is unsuitable for the purposes generally targeted by XAI. To illustrate this, we will use the following easy running example throughout this work.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 1.1</span></span><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Breakfast and diabetes)<span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Suppose blood glucose <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is measured in a patient to assess whether he or she has diabetes (<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=1</annotation></semantics></math>) or not (<math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=0</annotation></semantics></math>). Unfortunately, the patient ignores the doctor’s request not to eat breakfast before the test. We assume that the measured value <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> follows the (simplified) relation</span></p>
<table id="S1.EGx3" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S1.E3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S1.E3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle G=85+0.4\cdot C+40\cdot Y+U\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mn>85</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mi>U</mi></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle G=85+0.4\cdot C+40\cdot Y+U\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(3)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the carbohydrate intake during breakfast and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>U</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U</annotation></semantics></math> denotes independent noise. We assume that <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.15)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.15</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.15)</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\sim\mathcal{N}(60;25)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>25</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\sim\mathcal{N}(60;25)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.Thmtheorem1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U\sim\mathcal{N}(0;10)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>U</mi><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U\sim\mathcal{N}(0;10)</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">The graph in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> illustrates the causal structure of Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>: the value of <math id="S1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is caused by the variables <math id="S1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>. The plot on the left of Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> shows the Shapley values (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) for data generated from this simple problem. A red color indicates a high value of a feature, whereas a blue color indicates a low value. A positive Shapley value <math id="S1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(G)=\frac{1}{2}I_{\emptyset}(G)+\frac{1}{2}I_{C}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(G)=\frac{1}{2}I_{\emptyset}(G)+\frac{1}{2}I_{C}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a positive association whereas larger <math id="S1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> tend to co-occur with larger <math id="S1.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> if <math id="S1.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is either considered alone (<math id="S1.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math>) or in the context of a fixed <math id="S1.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (<math id="S1.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{C\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{C\}</annotation></semantics></math>). As red points in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> are concentrated on the right for <math id="S1.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we could conclude that high <math id="S1.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> values makes diabetes more likely, which matches intuition. We call this <em id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">positive relevance</em> in the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">The Shapley values for <math id="S1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>, on the other hand, indicate a <em id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">negative relevance</em>: high values of <math id="S1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> are associated with a lower diabetes probability. Does this imply that a single high carbohydrate intake makes it less likely that the patient has diabetes? This seems absurd and rightly so. The underlying reason is instead something which one might dub the “explain away effect” but which is typically known as <em id="S1.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">suppression</em> in the literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conger, 1974</a>]</cite>: When the patient consumed many carbohydrates there is no need for diabetes to explain high values of <math id="S1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
The presence of suppression in our example can be read off the causal graph in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>: the suppressor <math id="S1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is connected to the target <math id="S1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> via a node <math id="S1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> with two incoming arrows (known as a <em id="S1.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collider</em>). Conditioning on a collider leads to a spurious association, known as <em id="S1.p5.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collider bias</em>, which makes <math id="S1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> a suppressor of <math id="S1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. Collider bias and suppression are in this work considered as two sides of the same coin, cf. Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a>.
Not every feature attributed negative relevance by <math id="S1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>ϕ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi</annotation></semantics></math> will automatically be a suppressor. In other examples a feature can, of course, be of negative relevance as it really causes the target to decrease. Apparently, the “right” interpretation of a chart like the left one in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> can vary from situation to situation. This ambiguity can easily lead to wrong conclusions and undermines the usability of XAI for purposes such as model analysis, let alone for scientific discovery.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><span class="ltx_inline-block"><svg id="S1.F1.sf1.pic1" class="ltx_picture ltx_centering" height="27.9" overflow="visible" version="1.1" viewBox="0 0 184.89 27.9" width="184.89"><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="translate(0,27.9) matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 0) translate(92.39,0) translate(0,13.95)"><g stroke-width="0.4pt"><path style="fill:none" d="M 13.67 0 C 13.67 7.55 7.55 13.67 0 13.67 C -7.55 13.67 -13.67 7.55 -13.67 0 C -13.67 -7.55 -7.55 -13.67 0 -13.67 C 7.55 -13.67 13.67 -7.55 13.67 0 Z M 0 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -5.43 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.78em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.86"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.pic1.1" class="ltx_text">G</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -65.37 0 C -65.37 7.38 -71.36 13.37 -78.74 13.37 C -86.12 13.37 -92.11 7.38 -92.11 0 C -92.11 -7.38 -86.12 -13.37 -78.74 -13.37 C -71.36 -13.37 -65.37 -7.38 -65.37 0 Z M -78.74 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -83.74 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.72em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="9.99"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.pic1.2" class="ltx_text">C</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 92.23 0 C 92.23 7.45 86.19 13.49 78.74 13.49 C 71.29 13.49 65.25 7.45 65.25 0 C 65.25 -7.45 71.29 -13.49 78.74 -13.49 C 86.19 -13.49 92.23 -7.45 92.23 0 Z M 78.74 0"></path><g style="--ltx-stroke-color:#000000;--ltx-fill-color:#000000;" fill="#000000" stroke="#000000" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 73.55 -4.73)"><foreignObject style="--ltx-fo-width:0.75em;--ltx-fo-height:0.68em;--ltx-fo-depth:0em;font-size:10pt;" height="9.46" overflow="visible" transform="matrix(1 0 0 -1 0 9.46)" width="10.38"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_container"><span class="ltx_foreignobject_content"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.pic1.3" class="ltx_text">Y</span></span></span></foreignObject></g><path style="fill:none" d="M 64.98 0 L 14.59 0"></path><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.32pt" transform="matrix(-1.0 0.0 0.0 -1.0 14.59 0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21"></path></g><path style="fill:none" d="M -65.1 0 L -14.59 0"></path></g><g stroke-dasharray="none" stroke-dashoffset="0.0pt" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" stroke-width="0.32pt" transform="matrix(1.0 0.0 0.0 1.0 -14.59 0)"><path style="fill:none" d="M -1.66 2.21 C -1.52 1.38 0 0.14 0.42 0 C 0 -0.14 -1.52 -1.38 -1.66 -2.21"></path></g></g></svg></span>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal graph for Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a></span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S1.F1.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20396v1/figures/Shapley_values_diabetes_breakfast_Y.png" id="S1.F1.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/137;" width="476" height="137" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S1.F1.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Shapley (left) and cc-Shapley values (right) for Ex. <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a></span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Causal graph for Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> (top), together with the according results for conventional Shapley values (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) (bottom - left) and the cc-Shapley values (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) (bottom - right). Experimental details are in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>.
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Why were we able to tell that a suppression effect rather than a potential health benefit of one carbohydrate-rich breakfast is a more likely interpretation for the negative relevance of <math id="S1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>? We had to use something that a purely data-driven model does not have: an understanding of the real world, encoded in the causal diagram in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a>. In this article we propose to use this understanding for our treatment of the context variables <math id="S1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) via techniques from <em id="S1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">causal inference</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>]</cite>. This causal modification of (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is called <em id="S1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">cc-Shapley</em> values, and is introduced in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>. For Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we depict the cc-Shapley values on the right side of Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>. We observe that the cc-Shapley values do not attribute <math id="S1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> any importance for diabetes risk, which matches the intuition.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p7" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p7.1" class="ltx_p">The main contributions of this article are as follows:</p>
<ul id="S1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We highlight a fundamental problem underlying non-causal XAI methods such as (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). In causal inference, this problem is known as collider bias or suppression.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We propose cc-Shapley values, which modify (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) to use causal information. This is, to the best of our knowledge, the first approach designed to avoid collider bias without the need to restrict oneself to univariate feature importance.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We present theoretical and experimental results regarding the computability and behavior of cc-Shapley values in the presence of collider bias. We use several synthetic and one real world example.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with the literature.</h4>

<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">There are a plethora of XAI methods in the literature, cf. for example the reviews by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelov et al. [2021]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Minh et al. [2022]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Mersha et al. [2024]</a></cite>. While this work focuses entirely on Shapley values, the criticism raised here goes beyond this specific method. Typically, non-causal methods use only observational ingredients: the data and/or the model (which is also trained from the data). As causal information is not recoverable from observations alone in general and as collider bias is a causal phenomenon, it is implausible that any combination of purely observational information can eradicate the influence of collider bias.
Indeed, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilming et al. [2023]</a></cite> demonstrate the susceptibility of many popular XAI methods to suppression.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">There is a growing awareness that the current approach to XAI needs scrutiny or even revision
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Freiesleben and König, 2023</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al., 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilming et al., 2022</a>]</cite> with some works discussing the need and possibility for combining XAI with causal concepts, e.g., <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Carloni et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Beckers, 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karimi, 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holzinger et al., 2019</a>, <a href="#bib.bib17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schölkopf, 2022</a>]</cite>.
Various works propose using the underlying causal structure to produce counterfactual explanations, see, e.g., the works by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karimi et al. [2021]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Karimi et al. [2020]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib13" title="" class="ltx_ref">König et al. [2023]</a></cite>. The focus in these works is mostly to produce counterfactuals that are coherent with the actual world, but the topic of suppression is not discussed in this context.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janzing et al. [2020]</a>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">janzing2024quantifying</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">heskes2020causal</span></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">jung2022measuring</span></cite> also discuss Shapley values and the concept of intervention. Their approaches are, however, not usable for cases such as Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> as their methods either still attribute relevance to the suppressor or no relevance to any feature in Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> (cf. Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilming et al. [2023]</a></cite> study Shapley values for a simple suppressor problem for which no relevance is attributed to a suppressor; this statement is however only true for their considered example and a specific approximation to Eq. (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>). <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al. [2014]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. [2025]</a></cite> discuss the problem of suppression and observe that a univariate approach to feature importance avoids these problems. We will discuss the relation of our work to this idea in Section <a href="#S2.SS2" title="2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.2</span></a> including a discussion why univariate importance is in general not sufficient to assess the relevance of a feature.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Background</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Basics of Causal Inference</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We here recall core concepts of causal inference needed in this article and refer to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl [2009]</a></cite>. In causal inference, the data genererating process behind a set of variables is described by a <em id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">structural causal model (SCM)</em>, which is a triplet <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{V},(f_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}},(U_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}=(\mathcal{V},(f_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}},(U_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}})</annotation></semantics></math>, where</p>
<ul id="S2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is the set of variables,</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> is a set of independent<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                    <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                    
                    
                    
                  Throughout this work, we assume that the SCM satisfies the local Markov assumption, i.e., there are no hidden confounders outside the set <math id="footnote2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></span></span> random variables, representing noise, and</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">each variable <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> is related to its <em id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">parents</em> <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pa}_{X}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\backslash\{X\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>pa</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pa}_{X}\subseteq\mathcal{V}\backslash\{X\}</annotation></semantics></math> and its noise variable <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="U_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">U_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> via an <em id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">assignment function</em> <math id="S2.I1.i3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X=f_{X}(\mathrm{pa}_{X},U_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>pa</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X=f_{X}(\mathrm{pa}_{X},U_{X})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">To avoid inconsistencies, the relations are assumed to be acyclic: That is, when drawing arrows from <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{pa}_{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>pa</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{pa}_{X}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> for each <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, we arrive at a directed acyclic graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}=(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{E})</annotation></semantics></math> with edges <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math>. Given an instance of the noise variables <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(U_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(U_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math>, we can find the values of all <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> through evaluation of the assignment functions, starting from the source nodes without parents. The graph <math id="S2.SS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is called the <em id="S2.SS1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">causal graph</em> and represents the causal functional dependencies underlying the data generating process. Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> depicts the causal graph for Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Statistical association between variables can be assessed by studying the paths in <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. A <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">path</em> between two variables is a concatenation of adjacent edges from <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{E}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℰ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{E}</annotation></semantics></math> (independent of the direction of the edges) leading from one variable to the other. If all edges in a path have the same direction, we denote it by <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dashrightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dashrightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\dashleftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\dashleftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> and call it a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">causal path</em>. If a causal path consists of only one edge we call it <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">direct</em>, otherwise <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">indirect</em>.
A path between two sets <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}^{\prime}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}^{\prime}</annotation></semantics></math> is called a <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">backdoor path</em> if it leaves <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> with an incoming edge.
A path is called <em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">blocked</em>, conditional on a (possibly empty) set <math id="S2.SS1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}\subseteq\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}\subseteq\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math>, if it</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">contains a <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Z\in\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Z\in\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> that acts on this path as a <em id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">chain</em> <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow Z\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow Z\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> ( or <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow Z\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow Z\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math>) or as a <em id="S2.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">fork</em> <math id="S2.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow Z\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>Z</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow Z\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math>, or</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">if it contains a <em id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collider</em> <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow C\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow C\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> which is neither contained in <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> nor in the <em id="S2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">ancestors</em> <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{an}(\mathcal{Z})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{an}(\mathcal{Z})</annotation></semantics></math> of <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math> (the set of nodes that have a causal path leading to <math id="S2.I2.i2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>).</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">A path is called <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">unblocked</em> if it is not blocked. If all paths between two sets of variables <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S},\mathcal{S}^{\prime}\subseteq\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>′</mo></msup></mrow><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S},\mathcal{S}^{\prime}\subseteq\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> are blocked conditional on <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>, we call them <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">d-separated</em> and write <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}\mathcal{S}^{\prime}|\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}\mathcal{S}^{\prime}|\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. d-separation implies independence: <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathcal{S}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}\mathcal{S}^{\prime}|\mathcal{Z}\Rightarrow\mathcal{S}\perp\!\!\!\perp\mathcal{S}^{\prime}|\mathcal{Z}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>′</mo></msup><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒵</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}\mathcal{S}^{\prime}|\mathcal{Z}\Rightarrow\mathcal{S}\perp\!\!\!\perp\mathcal{S}^{\prime}|\mathcal{Z}</annotation></semantics></math>. The graph <math id="S2.SS1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math> is called <em id="S2.SS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">faithful</em> if the reverse direction is always true.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S2.SS1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Intervention</em> is the process of creating a new SCM from <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by setting a variable <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> to a specific value <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>x</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">x</annotation></semantics></math>, replacing <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{X}(\mathrm{pa}_{X},U_{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>pa</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{X}(\mathrm{pa}_{X},U_{X})</annotation></semantics></math> by <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{f}_{X}=x" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>f</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>X</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{f}_{X}=x</annotation></semantics></math>.
Intervention simulates the effect of a variable when we cut off its usual causes and corresponds to deleting all incoming arrows to <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>X</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>. We write <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X=x)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>x</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X=x)</annotation></semantics></math> or simply <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X)</annotation></semantics></math> for this operation and <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(X)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(X)}</annotation></semantics></math> for the modified SCM. Similarly, we can define an intervention <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{X})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{X})</annotation></semantics></math> on a subset of variables <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}\subseteq\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> or a stochastic intervention <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{X}\sim q)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{X}\sim q)</annotation></semantics></math> when drawing the values <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{X}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒳</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{X}</annotation></semantics></math> from a distribution <math id="S2.SS1.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> instead of using constant values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In supervised machine learning, the typical task is to estimate a target variable from other variables. To account for this distinction in our setup, we call one variable <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\in\mathcal{V}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\in\mathcal{V}</annotation></semantics></math> the <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">target</em> and the remaining variables <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{V}\backslash\{Y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\mathcal{V}\backslash\{Y\}</annotation></semantics></math> <em id="S2.SS1.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">features</em>. The features will be indexed as <math id="S2.SS1.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\{X_{i}:\,1\leq i\leq n\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mn> 1</mn><mo>≤</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>≤</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\{X_{i}:\,1\leq i\leq n\}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Collider bias</h3>

<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Suppressor Variables and Collider Bias.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a>, we observed that even for simple problems, uninformative features, i.e., features that are independent of the target, can be attributed importance by XAI methods. This is because these features might be useful to remove variance from informative features. In the literature, variables that are useful in such a secondary sense are known as <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">suppressor variables</em>, as they can be used to suppress noise <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Horst and others, 1941</a>, <a href="#bib.bib49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Conger, 1974</a>, <a href="#bib.bib51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Darlington, 1968</a>, <a href="#bib.bib50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kim, 2019</a>]</cite>. These references all give different definitions of what a suppressor is, partially contradicting each other or giving definitions that only make sense in a linear framework. We here follow <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilming et al. [2022]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al. [2024]</a></cite> and define suppression through <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collider bias</em>, the statistical association that arises due to the unblocking of paths when conditioning on a collider or its ancestors (cf. Section <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Basics of Causal Inference ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). We call a (possibly informative) feature a <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">suppressor</em> if it is connected to the target via paths that contain colliders. In this view, suppression and collider bias are just two sides of the same coin: suppressors are those variables whose association with the target might be affected by collider bias when conditioning on other variables.
Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> with the causal graph from Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf1" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(a)</span></a> is a very easy example on how the collider <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> makes <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> a suppressor. For general setups and a singleton <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{X_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{X_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math> the middle column of Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes how conditioning on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> can lead to collider bias between a feature <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and the target, making <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> a suppressor. The rows of Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> distinguish various types of paths between <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. In cells marked in red, conditioning on <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> will (potentially) lead to collider bias between <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>: previously blocked paths might become unblocked, inducing statistical associations along these paths. As the number of paths quickly grows with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}|</annotation></semantics></math>, the consequences of spurious association introduced by such effects is likely to become aggravated even for low-dimensional problems.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Univariate importance is insufficient.</h4>

<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The problem of suppressor variables and the susceptibility of many XAI methods to them is known in the XAI literature <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilming et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al., 2024</a>, <a href="#bib.bib41" title="" class="ltx_ref">König et al., 2025</a>, <a href="#bib.bib42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weichwald et al., 2015</a>]</cite>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al. [2014]</a></cite> observed that even the weights of linear models will highlight suppression variables as relevant. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. [2025]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gjølbye et al. [2025]</a></cite> extended this observation to generalized additive models and locally linear explanations. These three works also propose potential remedies for this problem, which could, in a nutshell, be summarized as (re-)fitting the considered feature <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to the target <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in a univariate fashion<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al. [2014]</a></cite> actually propose a method that, under the assumption of Gaussianity, is equivalent to a univariate linear regression from target to feature. The regression coefficient of such a fit equals, up to standard deviations, a linear univariate regression from feature to target.</span></span></span>. In the notation of (<a href="#S1.E2" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>), we can express such an approach as</p>
<table id="S2.EGx4" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S2.E4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S2.E4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]-\mathbb{E}[Y]\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]-\mathbb{E}[Y]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(4)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where we recall that the conditional expectation is the optimal fit of <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> onto <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Appendix <a href="#A2.SS1" title="B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a>. The object (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) is not susceptible to collider bias and thus to suppression as it does not condition on other features. In particular, we have
<math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp Y\Rightarrow I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.518em;" width="0.518em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp Y\Rightarrow I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})=0</annotation></semantics></math> so that (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) only highlights features that are statistically associated with <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, a property called the <em id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">statistical association property</em> (SAP) by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Clark et al. [2025]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilming et al. [2023]</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Haufe et al. [2024]</a></cite>. For Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, the SAP guarantees that <math id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(C)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(C)=0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Univariate importance measures such as (<a href="#S2.E4" title="In Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>) avoid attributing importance to statistically irrelevant features which is necessary to apply XAI for model debugging and scientific discovery. However, the interplay between variables is vital to the performance of a ML model and interactions of variables can create information that is not available by looking at each variable on its own as shown by</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_example">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Example 2.1</span></span><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Univariate importance is not enough)<span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Let <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1},X_{2}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\frac{1}{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1},X_{2}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(\frac{1}{2})</annotation></semantics></math> be independent and <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y:=X_{1}\cdot X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>:=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y:=X_{1}\cdot X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(X_{1})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{2})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(X_{1})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{2})=0</annotation></semantics></math> indicate no importance of the features whereas the “higher order” importance terms <math id="S2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{X_{2}}(X_{1})=X_{1}X_{2}-\frac{1}{2}X_{2},\,I_{X_{1}}(X_{2})=X_{1}X_{2}-\frac{1}{2}X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{X_{2}}(X_{1})=X_{1}X_{2}-\frac{1}{2}X_{2},\,I_{X_{1}}(X_{2})=X_{1}X_{2}-\frac{1}{2}X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> (correctly) indicate importance.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">A more complete XAI method will thus have to consider the multivariate interplay between variables. As we saw above, doing this purely based on observational data as done by mainstream approaches can lead to spurious association that can easily be misinterpreted. In the following section we demonstrate that using causal information provides a way out of this dilemma.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.T1" class="ltx_table">
<table id="S2.T1.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_centering ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S2.T1.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:106.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Paths </span><math id="S2.T1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> between </span><math id="S2.T1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.1.1.1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S2.T1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\ldots|X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo fence="false" mathsize="1.110em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\ldots|X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S2.T1.3.1.2.4" class="ltx_text"></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.1.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.1" class="ltx_text"></span><span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.3.1" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_tr">
<span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><math id="S2.T1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></span></span>
</span></span><span id="S2.T1.3.1.3.4" class="ltx_text"></span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.2" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.2.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.2.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:106.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.2.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.T1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow X_{k}\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow X_{k}\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.2.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> exists on </span><math id="S2.T1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.2.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.2.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  or </span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓<math id="S2.T1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.2.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.2.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  or </span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓<math id="S2.T1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.2.3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.3" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.3.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.3.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:106.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.T1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow X_{k}\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow X_{k}\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> exists on </span><math id="S2.T1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.3.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.3.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.2.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  or </span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓<math id="S2.T1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.3.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.3.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">  or </span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓<math id="S2.T1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math></span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.3.3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.4" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.4.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.4.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:106.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.T1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow X_{k}\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow X_{k}\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.4.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> exists on </span><math id="S2.T1.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.4.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.4.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;"><span id="S2.T1.3.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span> <math id="S2.T1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;" mathbackground="#FFBFBF" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.T1.3.4.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span>  or <span id="S2.T1.3.4.2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.4.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.4.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span><span id="S2.T1.3.4.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="S2.T1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.4.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.4.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.5" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.5.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.5.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:106.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.5.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><math id="S2.T1.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rightarrow C\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">→</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rightarrow C\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.5.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> exists on </span><math id="S2.T1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tau" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tau</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.5.1.1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> and </span><math id="S2.T1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\notin\tau,C\in\mathrm{an}(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo mathsize="0.900em">∉</mo><mi mathsize="0.900em">τ</mi></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">,</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">C</mi><mo mathsize="0.900em">∈</mo><mrow><mi mathsize="0.900em">an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><msub><mi mathsize="0.900em">X</mi><mi mathsize="0.900em">k</mi></msub><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\notin\tau,C\in\mathrm{an}(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.5.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.5.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;"><span id="S2.T1.3.5.2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span> <math id="S2.T1.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-bg-color:#FFBFBF;" mathbackground="#FFBFBF" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S2.T1.3.5.2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span>  or <span id="S2.T1.3.5.2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span></span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.5.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.5.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span><span id="S2.T1.3.5.3.2" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><math id="S2.T1.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\Rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo mathsize="0.900em" stretchy="false">⇒</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\Rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S2.T1.3.5.3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;"> </span><span id="S2.T1.3.5.3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S2.T1.3.6" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S2.T1.3.6.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_middle ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.6.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_middle" style="width:106.7pt;">
<span id="S2.T1.3.6.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S2.T1.3.6.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">None of the above</span></span>
</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.6.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">no effect</span></td>
<td id="S2.T1.3.6.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb" style="padding-top:1.15pt;padding-bottom:1.15pt;"><span id="S2.T1.3.6.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">no effect</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption" style="font-size:90%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table">Table 1: </span>Change in the state of being blocked (<span id="S2.T1.21" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span>) or unblocked (<span id="S2.T1.22" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span>) for paths between feature <math id="S2.T1.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="S2.T1.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> when conditioning (middle column) or intervening (right column) on <math id="S2.T1.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. ”<span id="S2.T1.23" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#FF0000;">✗</span>  or <span id="S2.T1.24" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#00FF00;">✓</span>” means that the path is potentially unblocked - depending on other path details.
Conditioning on <math id="S2.T1.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> potentially unblocks previously blocked paths, which can lead to <em id="S2.T1.25" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">collider bias</em> (cells marked in red). This is avoided by causal interventions <math id="S2.T1.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math>.</figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Using the causal context</h3>

<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">A causal version of Shapley values.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We saw in Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> for our running Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> that, even in this simple case, objects such as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|C,G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|C,G]</annotation></semantics></math> are susceptible to the problem of spurious correlations, namely between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> conditional on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>.
We here propose to solve problems of this kind as described in the following.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">First, abandoning the symmetry between the considered features, we propose to distinguish in each scenario between</p>
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">1.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The variable whose importance is to be studied.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">2.</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The variable(s) that describe the background within which the importance of the variable from 1 is studied. We call these variables the <em id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">context</em>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2" class="ltx_p">The “paradoxical” behavior for Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> arose when studying the importance of the feature <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> (carbohydrate intake) in the context of a glucose value of <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The quantity <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|C,G]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|C,G]</annotation></semantics></math> considers the collider <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as an observation, which leads to the spurious correlation between the suppressor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. We here propose to apply <em id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">interventions</em> on the context variable(s) instead of conditioning on observed values.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_definition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Definition 3.1</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">We define the <em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">importance of a feature <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in the interventional context of features <math id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math></em> as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx5" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(5)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Using (<a href="#S3.E5" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>) we define causal context Shapley (<em id="S3.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_upright">cc-Shapley</em>) values as</span></p>
<table id="S3.EGx6" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="S3.E6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="S3.E6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{cc}(X_{j})=\!\!\!\!\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\!\!\!\!{\small\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}}I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.376em;" width="0.376em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒮</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒮</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{cc}(X_{j})=\!\!\!\!\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\!\!\!\!{\small\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}}I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(6)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Intervening on the context allows us to remove collider bias: For the case of a univariate context <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{X_{k}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{X_{k}\}</annotation></semantics></math>, the rightmost column of Table <a href="#S2.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes the effect of the intervention <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> on the blocking of paths between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. In contrast to a mere conditioning on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> (middle column), the intervention <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(X_{k})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(X_{k})</annotation></semantics></math> does not unblock previously blocked paths.
In fact, the same holds for general context variables <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>:
Since intervention removes incoming arrows to <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}</annotation></semantics></math>, the operation <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> does not lead to a conditioning on colliders.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the running Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, we obtain, from Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3 (Irrelevant context). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a>, <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Intervention equals observation). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> below and Appendix <a href="#A3.SS1" title="C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.1</span></a>, <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(C)}(G)=I_{C}(G)\simeq\sigma(\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C-109))-0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>109</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(C)}(G)=I_{C}(G)\simeq\sigma(\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C-109))-0.15</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(G)}(C)=I_{\emptyset}(C)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(G)}(C)=I_{\emptyset}(C)=0</annotation></semantics></math>. Note that the information on the suppressor <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is still used but now counts towards the importance of the informative variable <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> it suppresses. The uninformative suppressor feature <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is assigned no importance. This can be shown to hold in general for any non-informative feature whenever the underlying causal graph is faithful. Thus, the cc-Shapley values as defined in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> fulfill the SAP.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_proposition">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Proposition 3.2</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(SAP for Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Given a feature <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> with <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}Y</annotation></semantics></math> we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for any <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>. This implies that we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}Y\Rightarrow\phi_{cc}(X_{j})=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇒</mo><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}Y\Rightarrow\phi_{cc}(X_{j})=0</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="S3.Thmtheorem2.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math> as in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.1" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">This follows from the fact that an intervention on <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot unblock paths between <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, hence <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m4" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}Y|\,\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><msub><mo>⟂</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi></msub><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.337em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}}Y|\,\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp Y|\,\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mpadded style="width:0.518em;" width="0.518em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mphantom></mphantom><mo>⟂</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\perp\!\!\!\perp Y|\,\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Why not use <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(X_{j},\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(X_{j},\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> instead?</h4>

<div id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">At first sight, it might appear tempting to replace a symmetric object such as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> with a symmetric interventional analogue such as <math id="S3.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(X_{j},\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(X_{j},\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the approach studied by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">jung2022measuring</span></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">heskes2020causal</span></cite>
and fits into the original framework proposed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shapley and others [1953]</a></cite>, cf. Appendix <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>.
However, this would delete too much association: In an anti-causal setup such as the running Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, where there are no causal paths from the features towards the target, no feature would be attributed importance.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Estimation</h3>

<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Simple cases.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Objects such as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> can rather easily be learned from data via training a machine learning model of choice that predicts <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{X_{j}\}\cup\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{X_{j}\}\cup\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Conditional expectation as optimally trained model). ‣ B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> in the appendix. Interventional objects such as <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> from Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> are harder to obtain. But in some cases they match observational quantities as shown by the following two lemmas whose proofs are in Appendix <a href="#A2.SS2" title="B.2 Proofs of Lemmas and ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.3</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Irrelevant context)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that there are no causal paths <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow Y,X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow Y,X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the running Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>, Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3 (Irrelevant context). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> implies that <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(G)}(C)=I_{\emptyset}(C)=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(G)}(C)=I_{\emptyset}(C)=0</annotation></semantics></math> as there are no causal paths <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G\dashrightarrow C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G\dashrightarrow C</annotation></semantics></math> and hence <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is irrelevant as context for <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma 3.4</span></span><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Intervention equals observation)<span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\,\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\,\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, either</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(no backdoor paths)</em><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> there are no unblocked backdoor paths from </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or from </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="S3.I2.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(purely causal setup)</em><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> there are no causal paths </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\dashrightarrow X_{j},\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\dashrightarrow X_{j},\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and no confounders </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathcal{V}\backslash\left(\{Y,X_{j}\}\cup\mathcal{S}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathcal{V}\backslash\left(\{Y,X_{j}\}\cup\mathcal{S}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="S3.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="S3.I2.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then we have the identities <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The feature <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> in Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> has no backdoor paths so that Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Intervention equals observation). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> implies <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(C)}(G)=I_{C}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(C)}(G)=I_{C}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>. The same argument implies that for Example <a href="#S2.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 2.1 (Univariate importance is not enough). ‣ Univariate importance is insufficient. ‣ 2.2 Collider bias ‣ 2 Background ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a> we have <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}(X_{1})=I_{X_{2}}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}(X_{1})=I_{X_{2}}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{1})}(X_{2})=I_{X_{1}}(X_{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{1})}(X_{2})=I_{X_{1}}(X_{2})</annotation></semantics></math> and thus <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{cc}(X_{1})=\phi(X_{2})\neq 0,\phi_{cc}(X_{2})=\phi(X_{2})\neq 0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≠</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{cc}(X_{1})=\phi(X_{2})\neq 0,\phi_{cc}(X_{2})=\phi(X_{2})\neq 0</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Backdoor adjustment.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma B.5 (Version of backdoor adjustment). ‣ B.3 Backdoor adjustment ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a> in the appendix we provide a formula for the identification of <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> from observational data similar to the backdoor adjustment from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl [2009]</a></cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Using the SCM.</h4>

<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> summarizes how <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> can be computed given an SCM <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. This is the method that we use in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> below. We first isolate the joint marginal <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> of the context variables <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> within the original model <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then perform a stochastic intervention on the SCM to obtain <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}</annotation></semantics></math>. In this modified model, we can obtain estimates of the functions <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> via standard multivariate fitting using data-driven models, cf. Appendix <a href="#A2.SS4" title="B.4 Reasoning behind Algorithm ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.4</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Unless one is working with synthetic examples, the SCM will naturally not be known, as we also discuss in Section <a href="#S3.SS3" title="3.3 Limitations ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> below.
However, given a causal graph and a defined noise structure, the functional relations can be estimated from observational data by regressing each feature on its parents <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Peters et al., 2017</a>]</cite>. We apply this for the real world example in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> below.</p>
</div>
<figure id="algorithm1" class="ltx_float ltx_algorithm ltx_framed ltx_framed_top">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_float"><span id="algorithm1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Algorithm 1</span> </span>Compute <math id="algorithm1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> from SCM</figcaption>
<div id="algorithm1.4" class="ltx_listing ltx_lst_numbers_left ltx_framed ltx_framed_topbottom ltx_listing">
<div id="algorithm1.4.1" class="ltx_listingline">
<span id="algorithm1.4.1.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Input:</span> </span>SCM <math id="algorithm1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\in\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\in\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>,
context <math id="algorithm1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.2" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1" class="ltx_text"><span id="algorithm1.4.2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Output:</span> </span><math id="algorithm1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.3" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"># </span><span id="algorithm1.4.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Create modified model </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.4" class="ltx_listingline">Create sampler of marginal <math id="algorithm1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>q</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="algorithm1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.5" class="ltx_listingline">Construct <math id="algorithm1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.6" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"># </span><span id="algorithm1.4.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Fit ML models (NN, XGBoost,...) </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.7" class="ltx_listingline">Sample <math id="algorithm1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(X_{j},\mathcal{S},Y)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(X_{j},\mathcal{S},Y)</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="algorithm1.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.8" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> fit <math id="algorithm1.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algorithm1.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.9" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m15" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\leftarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\leftarrow</annotation></semantics></math> fit <math id="algorithm1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j},\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j},\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="algorithm1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.10" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter"># </span><span id="algorithm1.4.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Compute importance </span>
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.11" class="ltx_listingline"><math id="algorithm1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})\leftarrow\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})\leftarrow\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> ;
</div>
<div id="algorithm1.4.12" class="ltx_listingline"><span id="algorithm1.4.12.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">return</span> <math id="algorithm1.m19" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="algorithm1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>

</div>
</div>
</figure>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.3 </span>Limitations</h3>

<div id="S3.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Neither the original Shapley values (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) nor our causal analogue (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) are scalable in the way they are formulated here. The number of of context sets <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> increases swiftly with <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}|" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}|</annotation></semantics></math> and for each feature <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and context <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> two data-driven models have to be fitted to estimate <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m5" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S3.SS3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math>. Scalable approximations <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen et al., 2023</a>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">parafita2025practical</span>, <span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">teal2026exactly</span>]</cite> could be a way out but were not studied in the scope of this work. The main focus of this article is to highlight a systematic weakness in the existing approach to XAI and to point out what is necessary to fix it.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Another limitation that is shared by many other works on causality, is the assumption that the causal graph that has generated the data is known. Once we have obtained this graph, we can often fit the functions <math id="S3.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(f_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>X</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(f_{X})_{X\in\mathcal{V}}</annotation></semantics></math> in the SCM and subsequently apply Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. This is the procedure that we follow for the real world example in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> below.
In specific cases, such as linear SCMs with non-Gaussian noise, algorithms such as LiNGAM <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shimizu et al., 2011</a>]</cite> can be used, cf. Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> below, to obtain the causal graph. For more general approaches to causal discovery compare also the work of <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zheng et al. [2018]</a></cite> and the reviews by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zanga et al. [2022]</a>, <a href="#bib.bib47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Glymour et al. [2019]</a></cite>. In the general case, however, obtaining a valid causal graph is typically non-trivial and often requires expert knowledge. Moreover, in accordance with many other works in causal inference, we have to assume that the exogenous variables in our model are uncorrelated, which is typically an oversimplification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Finally, the usage of a causal graph assumes that a variable has a specific static meaning throughout the dataset, which is typically not true for other types of data such as images. Here causal representation learning becomes necessary to apply causal techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schölkopf et al., 2021</a>, <a href="#bib.bib19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Brehmer et al., 2022</a>, <a href="#bib.bib20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ahuja et al., 2023</a>]</cite>. We did not use such techniques here and only restrict ourselves to static data.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Experimental results</h2>

<figure id="S4.F2" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20396v1/figures/bivariate_importance_obs_scatter.png" id="S4.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/408;" width="476" height="408" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F2.fig2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><img src="2602.20396v1/figures/bivariate_importance_int_scatter.png" id="S4.F2.g2" class="ltx_graphics ltx_img_square" style="aspect-ratio:476/408;" width="476" height="408" alt="Refer to caption">
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S4.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Comparison of the regression coefficients <math id="S4.F2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|X_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" mathsize="1.580em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|X_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> (left) and <math id="S4.F2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false" mathsize="1.580em">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> (right) with <math id="S4.F2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for randomly sampled linear SCMs with 9 variables. The color encodes the extent to which <math id="S4.F2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acts as collider, cf. Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Linear SCM experiment ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Linear SCMs.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We randomly sample 3,000 linear SCMs with 8 features, i.e., <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{8}\},\,\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{F}\cup\{Y\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>8</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{8}\},\,\mathcal{V}=\mathcal{F}\cup\{Y\}</annotation></semantics></math>, and additive non-Gaussian (Laplacian) noise as described in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Linear SCM experiment ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>. For each SCM, the effect of including univariate context <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}=\{X_{2}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}=\{X_{2}\}</annotation></semantics></math> on the importance of the variable <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> is studied.
Using linear models for the fitting of conditional expectations and for Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>, we obtain partial regression coefficients <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|X_{2}},b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})},b_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|X_{2}},b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})},b_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> for the <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>-dependency of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{X_{2}}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{X_{2}}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(X_{1})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(X_{1})</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> compares <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|X_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|X_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> (left) and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> (right) for all sampled SCMs. Each point represents a different linear SCM. For the computation of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> we used the DirectLiNGAM algorithm from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shimizu et al. [2011]</a></cite> to identify the causal graph, so that no prior knowledge about the causal structure was used. The color of each point represents how many of the paths between <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> that run through <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> are blocked due to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> acting as a collider. This is measured by a heuristic quantity described in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS2" title="C.2 Linear SCM experiment ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.2</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For yellow points, almost all paths between <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> running through <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> contain <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> as a collider, so that we expect the collider bias to be most distinct for these points. We observe that, in the comparison <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|X_{2}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|X_{2}}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> (left), these points are usually placed far away from the diagonal, indicating that including the observed value of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> did change the importance of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. For the interventional comparison <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}|\mathrm{do}(X_{2})}</annotation></semantics></math> vs. <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="b_{X_{1}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>b</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">b_{X_{1}}</annotation></semantics></math> (right), these points lie on the diagonal and hence context <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{2}</annotation></semantics></math> that mostly affects <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math> via collider bias does not lead to a change in importance attributed to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Nonlinear case.</h4>

<figure id="S4.F3" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2602.20396v1/figures/Shapley_values_diabetes_risk_Y.png" id="S4.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/161;" width="476" height="161" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S4.F3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Shapley (left) and cc-Shapley (right) values for the nonlinear diabetes example considered in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Consider again the task of predicting whether a patient has type 2 diabetes (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=1</annotation></semantics></math>) or not (<math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y=0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y=0</annotation></semantics></math>). We assume this time that the patient did obey and not eat breakfast, but measure in addition to the blood glucose <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> also the average blood sugar <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and the BMI <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> of the patient.
The causal graph for this example is shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>, the (synthetic) SCM is specified in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Diabetes and BMI example ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">It is widely accepted that a high BMI <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is associated with an increased risk of type 2 diabetes <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chandrasekaran and Weiskirchen, 2024</a>]</cite>.
When looking at the Shapley values (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) shown on the left in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, we might come to a different conclusion: The BMI <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> is attributed a negative relevance for diabetes, which one could easily misinterpret as “high BMI leads to a low diabetes risk”. Using cc-Shapley values (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) instead leads to the values depicted on the right in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. The importance behavior of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> (and the other features) now matches intuition. This different behavior of Shapley and cc-Shapley values arises, once more, due to collider bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The Shapley values for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in this example are computed as <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi(B)=\frac{1}{3}I_{\emptyset}(B)+\frac{1}{6}I_{G}(B)+\frac{1}{6}I_{H}(B)+\frac{1}{3}I_{\{G,H\}}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>6</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>H</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi(B)=\frac{1}{3}I_{\emptyset}(B)+\frac{1}{6}I_{G}(B)+\frac{1}{6}I_{H}(B)+\frac{1}{3}I_{\{G,H\}}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>.
As shown in the first plot in the top row of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, the context-free quantity <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> alone indicates a positive relevance for diabetes risk in line with our expectations. The overall negative relevance of large <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> must therefore stem from the terms with context. The left plot in the middle row of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> shows the values of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> for various choices of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> as a heatmap. For <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> in a certain range, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> indicates a negative relevance of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. A similar behavior can be observed for <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>, cf. Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Diabetes and BMI example ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>. A look at the causal graph in Figure <a href="#S4.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a> reveals that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> act as colliders between <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> so that conditioning on <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\{G,H\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\{G,H\}</annotation></semantics></math> will create a spurious (here negative) association with <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> leading to the pattern observed in Figure <a href="#S4.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F4" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="S4.F4.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="S4.F4.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S4.F4.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;" rowspan="2"><span id="S4.F4.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text">
<span id="S4.F4.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_transformed_outer" style="width:6.6pt;height:44.1pt;vertical-align:-0.0pt;"><span class="ltx_transformed_inner" style="width:44.1pt;transform:translate(-18.8pt,-18.8pt) rotate(-90deg) ;">
<span id="S4.F4.3.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.F4.3.1.1.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">univariate</span></span>
</span></span></span></td>
<td id="S4.F4.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="S4.F4.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:75.9pt;">
