<span id="S4.F4.3.6.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:75.9pt;"><img src="2602.20396v1/figures/diabetes_risk_I_INT_Y_G_B.png" id="S4.F4.g6" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/357;" width="476" height="357" alt="Refer to caption">
</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S4.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Various terms arising in the computation of the conventional observational Shapley values (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and the cc-Shapley values (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) for the (more complex) diabetes example introduced in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. The upper row shows (with standard error) the context-free univariate term that coincides for (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">Once we use <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(G)}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(G)}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}(B)</annotation></semantics></math>, this bias is removed and we observe no negative relevance of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> in the context of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, as shown by the first plot in the third row of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Interestingly, when we switch the roles of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>, we obtain a different effect: Lemma <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Intervention equals observation). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a> implies that <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(B)}(G)=I_{B}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(B)}(G)=I_{B}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> as there are no backdoor paths to <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>. Both observational and interventional objects now match, cf. the second column of Figure <a href="#S4.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, and reveal a positive relevance of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math>. The reason for the context dependency of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(B)}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(B)}(G)</annotation></semantics></math> is that the relation <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B\rightarrow G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>G</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B\rightarrow G</annotation></semantics></math> is causal and knowing <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>
allows us to remove the variance in <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and thus judge the relation between <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> with more precision. This behavior resembles the observations of Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>: The suppression effect of <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> on <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> is now only accounted for in the relevance of the suppressed variable <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and does not affect the importance of the suppressor <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math>.
In fact, we have <math id="S4.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{cc}(B)=I_{\emptyset}(B)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{cc}(B)=I_{\emptyset}(B)</annotation></semantics></math> as we show in Appendix <a href="#A3.SS3" title="C.3 Diabetes and BMI example ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F5" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
