<section id="A1" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix A </span>On the used version of Shapley values</h2>

<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Comparison with the literature.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the context of game theory, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shapley and others [1953]</a></cite> introduced a general expression of the form</p>
<table id="A1.EGx7" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi(X_{j})=\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\gamma(\mathcal{S})(v(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})-v(\mathcal{S}))\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi(X_{j})=\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\gamma(\mathcal{S})(v(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})-v(\mathcal{S}))\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(7)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathcal{S})={\small\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒮</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">𝒮</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">−</mo><mn mathsize="0.900em">1</mn></mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic" mathsize="0.900em">ℱ</mi><mo maxsize="0.900em" minsize="0.900em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo mathsize="0.900em">!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathcal{S})={\small\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v:2^{\mathcal{F}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi>ℝ</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v:2^{\mathcal{F}}\rightarrow\mathbb{R}</annotation></semantics></math> is a general set function that satisfies some simple properties and should be thought of as encoding a game. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipovetsky and Conklin [2001]</a></cite> use <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="R^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>R</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">R^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> for <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in the context of linear regression to judge feature importance. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Datta et al. [2016]</a></cite> consider a more general framework that uses (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In Comparison with the literature. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) to measure the influence of inputs with various choices of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math>. Referring to the works above, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee [2017]</a></cite> formulate a “classic” version of Shapley values as</p>
<table id="A1.EGx8" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\phi(X_{j})=\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\gamma(\mathcal{S})(f(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})-f(\mathcal{S}))\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi(X_{j})=\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\gamma(\mathcal{S})(f(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})-f(\mathcal{S}))\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(8)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">here <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> denote functions that were trained only on the features <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> respectively. The optimally trained models <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>f</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f</annotation></semantics></math> have precise mathematical formulations in terms of a conditional expectation, cf. Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Conditional expectation as optimally trained model). ‣ B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> below. We can thus identify <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m11" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="f(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S},X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{S}\cup\{X_{j}\})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S},X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{S})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{S})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> and arrive at the expression (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) used in this work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Expression (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) is model agnostic in the sense that it only uses optimal models and not a specific instance of a trained model. While <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lundberg and Lee [2017]</a></cite> refer to classical Shapley values as formulated in (<a href="#A1.E8" title="In Comparison with the literature. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>), their proposed algorithm SHAP completely builds on the full model <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{F})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{F})</annotation></semantics></math>. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><a href="#bib.bib14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Janzing et al. [2020]</a></cite> explicitly use the output of the model, called <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following, for their arguments and their algorithm. The approach presented in this work is also usable for <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>.
A simplified, pragmatic adaption might be to treat <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> as an estimate of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, which is likely to yield comparable results for models with high accuracy (or low MSE in the case of regression). A more causal approach would be to add a node <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> to the causal graph and draw arrows from each feature used for training to <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> with the corresponding assignment function <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f_{\hat{Y}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>f</mi><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f_{\hat{Y}}</annotation></semantics></math> now just given by the learned machine learning model. Doing so will however not cure the problems inherent to (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that we outlined in the main text: Consider again the example with the causal diagram <a href="#S4.F5.sf1" title="In Figure 5 ‣ Nonlinear case. ‣ 4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5(a)</span></a>. First, note that <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> can actually not simply be removed from the diagram as it acts as a mediator (a chain on a causal path) between <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> and the nodes <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math>. Second, there is a collider bias between <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="B" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>B</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">B</annotation></semantics></math> when conditioning on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="G" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>G</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">G</annotation></semantics></math> so that even with <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> completely replaced by <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{Y}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mover accent="true"><mi>Y</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{Y}</annotation></semantics></math> we would have terms in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) that are influenced by collider bias.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Summarizing, we believe that the model-agnostic approach presented in this work is the most suitable and expressive to highlight feature relevance, as it uses the actual process underlying the data and simplifies matters by ignoring model specific aspects.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On the combinatorial coefficients <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We mentioned above that there are various versions of Shapley values. These versions only differ in their choice of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="v" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>v</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">v</annotation></semantics></math> in (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) but keep the same coefficients <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. We here shortly recall the meaning of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Osborne and Rubinstein, 1994</a>]</cite> to motivate why we also use them in the cc-Shapley values from Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>:</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">If we do not want to make any additional assumptions, it is unclear which features <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> to use as a context to judge the relevance of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. To decide this in a fair manner, imagine that we base our decision on a randomized experiment: We shuffle the order of features <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">…</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}=\{X_{1},\ldots,X_{n}\}</annotation></semantics></math> by uniformly drawing from all permutations on <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. We then use all variables that come before <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> in such a ordering as context. The probability that the set <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> is chosen as context in such an experiment is then just</p>
<table id="A1.EGx9" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\gamma(\mathcal{S})=\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\gamma(\mathcal{S})=\frac{|\mathcal{S}|!(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!}{|\mathcal{F}|!}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(9)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.2" class="ltx_p">Here, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{S}|!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{S}|!</annotation></semantics></math> are the number of ways we can reorder <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in front of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(|\mathcal{F}|-|\mathcal{S}|-1)!</annotation></semantics></math> are the number of ways we can order the remaining features after <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, and the denominator <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="|\mathcal{F}|!" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>!</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">|\mathcal{F}|!</annotation></semantics></math> is just the number of permutations in <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, we see that the coefficients in (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In Comparison with the literature. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) add up to 1.</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The same logic can be applied to motivate the usage of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> for the cc-Shapley values in Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a>: We choose a random subset <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> as causal context using the same randomized procedure.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">On the asymmetry of cc-Shapley values.</h4>

<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Definition <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem1" title="Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we introduce the cc-Shapley values as</p>
<table id="A1.EGx10" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A1.E10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A1.E10.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\phi_{cc}(X_{j})=\!\!\!\!\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\!\!\!\!\gamma(\mathcal{S})\,(\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})])\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mpadded style="width:0.376em;" width="0.376em"><mo>=</mo></mpadded><mstyle displaystyle="true"><munder><mo movablelimits="false">∑</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></munder></mstyle><mi>γ</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\phi_{cc}(X_{j})=\!\!\!\!\sum_{\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}}\!\!\!\!\gamma(\mathcal{S})\,(\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})])\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(10)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.2" class="ltx_p">We motivated above the usage of the same coefficients <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\gamma(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>γ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\gamma(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>. However, in one other fundamental aspect this definition differs from (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In Comparison with the literature. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>): The objects <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be understood as set functions as they treat <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> differently. Our cc-Shapley values therefore lack the symmetry in the treatment of all considered features and, in general, will not have a property such as <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sum_{j}\phi(X_{j})=v(\mathcal{F})-v(\emptyset)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mo>∑</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mrow><mi>ϕ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sum_{j}\phi(X_{j})=v(\mathcal{F})-v(\emptyset)</annotation></semantics></math> satisfied by (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In Comparison with the literature. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib23" title="" class="ltx_ref">Shapley and others, 1953</a>]</cite>. From a game theoretical viewpoint our asymmetric modification means that we give the “player” <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> an advantage that we don’t grant the other “players” <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, namely to use non-causal association. This asymmetric treatment is vital to the performance of <math id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\phi_{cc}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>ϕ</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\phi_{cc}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact,</p>
</div>
<div id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="A1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We need to allow for non-causal association between <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A1.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> to obtain non-trivial importance in anti-causal setups, cf. Section <a href="#S3" title="3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
<li id="A1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The context variables <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> cannot be treated in the same way, as we would otherwise allow <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> to be attributed importance that solely arises from suppressing noise in the variables <math id="A1.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as we observe for Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A1.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Breaking the symmetric, equal treatment of all features in (<a href="#A1.E7" title="In Comparison with the literature. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>) is therefore precisely what allows (<a href="#A1.E10" title="In On the asymmetry of cc-Shapley values. ‣ Appendix A On the used version of Shapley values ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>) to correct spurious associations.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A2" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix B </span>Further theoretical aspects</h2>

<section id="A2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.1 </span>Conditional expectation as optimal model</h3>

<div id="A2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following basic result recalls that the mathematically optimal model for a typical (supervised) machine learning problem is given by the conditional expectation. In particular, this shows that we can obtain an estimate of conditional expectations such as <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> with features <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}</annotation></semantics></math> simply by training a data-driven model with input <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and target <math id="A2.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> using MSE-loss (for regression) or cross-entropy loss (for classification).</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.1</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Conditional expectation as optimally trained model)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider a set of square-integrable random variables <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and another square integrable and real-valued random variable <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> defined on the same probability space. Then the optimization problem</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx11" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{arg}\,\mathrm{min}_{f}\mathbb{E}[|Y-f(\mathcal{S})|^{2}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{arg}\,\mathrm{min}_{f}\mathbb{E}[|Y-f(\mathcal{S})|^{2}]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(11)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">has (almost surely) a unique solution given by the conditional expectation <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math>. If <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is binary, then <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]=P(Y=1|\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]=P(Y=1|\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> is moreover the optimal solution to the problem</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathrm{arg}\,\mathrm{min}_{f}\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(\mathcal{S}))]\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>arg</mi><mo lspace="0.170em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>min</mi><mi>f</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathrm{arg}\,\mathrm{min}_{f}\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(\mathcal{S}))]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(12)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">where <math id="A2.Thmtheorem1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(S))=-Y\log f(\mathcal{S})-(1-Y)(1-\log f(\mathcal{S}))" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(S))=-Y\log f(\mathcal{S})-(1-Y)(1-\log f(\mathcal{S}))</annotation></semantics></math> is the cross-entropy loss.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_remark">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Remark B.2</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">A similar statement holds for the multivariate case or multiclass case (when <math id="A2.Thmtheorem2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is one-hot-encoded).</p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS1.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">The statement on <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L2" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>L</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L2</annotation></semantics></math>-optimality (<a href="#A2.E11" title="In Lemma B.1 (Conditional expectation as optimally trained model). ‣ B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a>) is a classical result from probability theory <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Loeve and Stone, 2013</a>]</cite>. The statement based on (<a href="#A2.E12" title="In Lemma B.1 (Conditional expectation as optimally trained model). ‣ B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>) can be seen by using the tower property of conditional expectations:</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx13" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex1"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(\mathcal{S}))]=\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(\mathcal{S}))|\mathcal{S}]]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℒ</mi><mi>CE</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(\mathcal{S}))]=\mathbb{E}[\mathbb{E}[\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{CE}}(Y;f(\mathcal{S}))|\mathcal{S}]]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex2"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}[p_{\mathcal{S}}\log f(\mathcal{S})+(1-p_{\mathcal{S}})(1-\log f(\mathcal{S}))]\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=-\mathbb{E}[p_{\mathcal{S}}\log f(\mathcal{S})+(1-p_{\mathcal{S}})(1-\log f(\mathcal{S}))]\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS1.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS1.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p_{\mathcal{S}}=P(Y=1|\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p_{\mathcal{S}}=P(Y=1|\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math>.
Minimizing the inner expression w.r.t <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> (setting the derivative to zero), yields <math id="A2.SS1.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="f(\mathcal{S})=p_{\mathcal{S}}=P(Y=1|\mathcal{S})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">f(\mathcal{S})=p_{\mathcal{S}}=P(Y=1|\mathcal{S})=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.2 </span>Proofs of Lemmas <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem3" title="Lemma 3.3 (Irrelevant context). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.3</span></a> and <a href="#S3.Thmtheorem4" title="Lemma 3.4 (Intervention equals observation). ‣ Simple cases. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.4</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We here restate the lemmas from Section <a href="#S3.SS2" title="3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.2</span></a> together with their proofs.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.3</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Irrelevant context)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> and assume that there are no causal paths <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow Y,X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow Y,X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Then we have <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem3.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">We apply rule 3 of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>]</cite> to remove <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> from the conditional expectation, i.e., we have to verify the following d-separation</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx14" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E13.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle Y\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}}\mathcal{S}\,\,|\,\,X_{j}\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo fence="false" lspace="0.340em" rspace="0.507em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}}\mathcal{S}\,\,|\,\,X_{j}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(13)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the graph where all incoming edges to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> were deleted.
The actual rule 3 uses <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}(X_{j})}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mover accent="true"><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}(X_{j})}}</annotation></semantics></math> instead of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math>, which denotes the removal of all ancestors of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> from <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. By assumption, <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> contains, however, no ancestors of <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Consider, conditional on <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, a potential unblocked path between <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> the last element within <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on the way to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and consider, from now on, the unblocked sub-path from <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. As we removed ingoing edges to <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>, this path must have an outgoing edge at <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. By assumption, there is no path <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> . The only remaining path with an outgoing edge at <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><mover accent="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></mover></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\overline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is unblocked conditional on <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> can be a path on which <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> or its ancestors <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{an}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{an}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> serve as colliders and there are no other colliders on this path. Let <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathrm{an}(X_{j})\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathrm{an}(X_{j})\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> denote the first of these colliders. We know that there is no other collider between <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and that the path leaves <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> with outgoing edge, we must thus have <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\dashrightarrow C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\dashrightarrow C</annotation></semantics></math> (the arrows cannot flip direction in between). However, this cannot be true as <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathrm{an}(X_{j})\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathrm{an}(X_{j})\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> would imply that there is a path <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\dashrightarrow X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\dashrightarrow X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> which violates our assumption. This shows (<a href="#A2.E13" title="In Proof. ‣ B.2 Proofs of Lemmas and ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a>) and hence <math id="A2.SS2.p2.m35" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j}]</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text">As we assumed that there are no causal paths <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> we have further <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y]</annotation></semantics></math> from which the identity <math id="A2.SS2.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{k})}(X_{j})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(X_{k})}(X_{j})=I_{\emptyset}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> follows.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.4</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Intervention equals observation)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\,\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\,\mathcal{S}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> such that, either</span></p>
<ul id="A2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(no backdoor paths)</em><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> there are no unblocked backdoor paths from </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or from </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> to </span><math id="A2.I1.i1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, or</span></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><em id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph">(purely causal setup)</em><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> there are no causal paths </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y\dashrightarrow X_{j},\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y\dashrightarrow X_{j},\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and no confounders </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H\in\mathcal{V}\backslash\left(\{Y,X_{j}\}\cup\mathcal{S}\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H\in\mathcal{V}\backslash\left(\{Y,X_{j}\}\cup\mathcal{S}\right)</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> with </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> or </span><math id="A2.I1.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">,</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then we have the identities <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.Thmtheorem4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=I_{\mathcal{S}}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>.</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.3" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text">We will show the identity for the conditional expectation first. For both scenarios, ”no backdoor paths” and ”purely causal setup” we will use rule 2 of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>]</cite> to replace the <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math>-operation by conditioning. We have to show</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx15" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E14.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle Y\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}}\mathcal{S}|X_{j}\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}}\mathcal{S}|X_{j}\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(14)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p4.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p4.2.1" class="ltx_text">where <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the graph where all outgoing edges from <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> were removed. Consider first the “no backdoor paths” scenario:
Any path between <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is unblocked conditional on <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> will have an incoming edge when leaving <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> (as it runs in <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math>) and either 1) not contain <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> or 2) contain <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> or ancestors of <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> as colliders and no other colliders. In the first case, we have just an unblocked backdoor path from <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and in the second case we can subtract an unblocked subpath which is a backdoor path between <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p4.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Both violate our assumption and therefore we conclude (<a href="#A2.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ B.2 Proofs of Lemmas and ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text">For the ”purely causal setup” scenario consider again a path between <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> in <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}</annotation></semantics></math> that is unblocked conditional on <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. Denote by <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> the last element of this path within <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> on the way to <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and consider from now on the unblocked subpath between <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Since we removed outgoing edges from <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math> this path can only have an incoming edge to <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}</annotation></semantics></math>. Due to our assumptions this path cannot be of the form <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\dashleftarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\dashleftarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The only remaining paths that are unblocked, conditional on <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m13" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>, have a fork. This fork cannot be <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m14" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\leftarrow X_{j}\rightarrow" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo stretchy="false">←</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mphantom></mphantom></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\leftarrow X_{j}\rightarrow</annotation></semantics></math> as it would be blocked conditional on <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m15" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math>. We also know, by assumption, that there is no <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> such that <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>. The arrows must thus flip directions before or after the fork, which means that there is a collider. The only way this cannot lead to a blocked path is that <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m18" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> or a set of ancestors of <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> are these colliders and there are no other colliders. We will write <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}\subseteq\{X_{j}\}\cup\mathrm{an}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}\subseteq\{X_{j}\}\cup\mathrm{an}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> for this set. Call <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m21" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> the last element in <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m22" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{C}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒞</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{C}</annotation></semantics></math> on the way of the considered path to <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m23" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. Since <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m24" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> is a collider, as there is no further collider between <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m25" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m26" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> on the path and as the remaining bit of the path between <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m27" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m28" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> is unblocked we must either have <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m29" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> for some <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m30" class="ltx_Math" alttext="H" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>H</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">H</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m31" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\dashleftarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\dashleftarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, depending on the direction of the last edge of the path. In either case we obtain due to <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m32" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C\in\{X_{j}\}\cup\mathrm{an}(X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mi>an</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C\in\{X_{j}\}\cup\mathrm{an}(X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math> that either <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m33" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS2.p5.m34" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\dashleftarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\dashleftarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, which both violate our assumptions. Hence, there is no unblocked path and (<a href="#A2.E14" title="In Proof. ‣ B.2 Proofs of Lemmas and ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>) follows.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text">We still have to show <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S}]</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows again from rule 2 of <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus once we know</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx16" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex3"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex3.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle Y\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}}\mathcal{S}\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mpadded style="width:0.240em;" width="0.240em"><mo>⟂</mo></mpadded><mo>⟂</mo><msub><mrow></mrow><msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒢</mi><munder accentunder="true"><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="true">¯</mo></munder></msub></msub><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y\perp\!\!\!\perp_{\mathcal{G}_{\underline{\mathcal{S}}}}\mathcal{S}\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS2.p6.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS2.p6.2.1" class="ltx_text">For the ”no backdoor paths” scenario this follows from the assumption that there are no backdoor paths from <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>. For the ”purely causal setup” scenario we can use that any unconditional unblocked backdoor path is either form <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math> or <math id="A2.SS2.p6.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇠</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashleftarrow H\dashrightarrow Y</annotation></semantics></math>, which are cases which we both excluded in our assumption.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.3 </span>Backdoor adjustment</h3>

<div id="A2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The following lemma provides an adaption of the “backdoor adjustment” <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>, Theorem 3.3.2]</cite> for the context of this work.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem5" class="ltx_theorem ltx_theorem_lemma">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_title_theorem"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_theorem"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Lemma B.5</span></span><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold"> </span>(Version of backdoor adjustment)<span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">.</span></h6>
<div id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Consider <math id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\,\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\},\mathcal{S}\cap\mathcal{W}=\emptyset" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi></mrow><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>⊆</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℱ</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo><mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∩</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}\in\mathcal{F},\,\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}\subseteq\mathcal{F}\backslash\{X_{j}\},\mathcal{S}\cap\mathcal{W}=\emptyset</annotation></semantics></math> such that</span></p>
<ul id="A2.I2" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="A2.I2.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">There are no causal paths </span><math id="A2.I2.i1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math></p>
</div></li>
<li id="A2.I2.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="A2.I2.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Both, </span><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">, block all backdoor paths between </span><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic"> and </span><math id="A2.I2.i2.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math><span id="A2.I2.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">.</span></p>
</div></li>
</ul>
<p id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.Thmtheorem5.p1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">then we have the identity</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx17" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex4"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex4.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|X_{j}}[\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}}[\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|X_{j}}[\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]-\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}}[\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.2" class="ltx_proof">
<h6 class="ltx_title ltx_runin ltx_font_italic ltx_title_proof">Proof.</h6>
<div id="A2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text">Recall that <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}(X_{j})=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]-\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math>. By the rules for conditional expectations, we have</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx18" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex5"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex5.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}),\mathcal{W}]]\mbox{ and }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext> and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}),\mathcal{W}]]\mbox{ and }</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex6"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex6.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}),\mathcal{W}]]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}),\mathcal{W}]]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.2" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.2.1" class="ltx_text">Since the sets <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> block all backdoor paths between <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> we can replace the inner <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> operation by conditioning (rule 2 of <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>]</cite>) and obtain</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx19" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex7"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex7.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]\mbox{ and }" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mtext> and</mtext></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]\mbox{ and }</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex8"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex8.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex8.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo fence="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{W}|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}[\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathcal{S},\mathcal{W}]]\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS3.p2.3" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS3.p2.3.1" class="ltx_text">To conclude, we remove the <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> intervention in the outer expectation using <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(X_{j},\mathcal{W}|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}))=P(X_{j},\mathcal{W})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(X_{j},\mathcal{W}|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}))=P(X_{j},\mathcal{W})</annotation></semantics></math>, which follows from the fact that there are no causal paths <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">⇢</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo>∪</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}\dashrightarrow\mathcal{W}\cup\{X_{j}\}</annotation></semantics></math> (rule 3 of <math id="A2.SS3.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>]</cite>).
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Application of Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem5" title="Lemma B.5 (Version of backdoor adjustment). ‣ B.3 Backdoor adjustment ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.5</span></a> requires specific conditions on the relation between <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{j}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{j}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> as well as a search for a suitable set <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. Once found, we have to find a reasonable estimate for the conditional probability <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(\mathcal{W}|X_{j})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(\mathcal{W}|X_{j})</annotation></semantics></math>, which can be a complicated task, especially for higher dimensional <math id="A2.SS3.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{W}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒲</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{W}</annotation></semantics></math>. In the experiments in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>, we therefore found a it more practical to first estimate the assignment functions in the SCM followed by the application of Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. Identification via more advanced techniques as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">teal2026exactly</span></cite> or <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_cite"><span class="ltx_ref ltx_missing_citation ltx_ref_self">jung2022measuring</span></cite> could be a feasible alternative but were not studied in the scope of this work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A2.SS4" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">B.4 </span>Reasoning behind Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a></h3>

<div id="A2.SS4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> implicitly claims that fitting data-driven models in the modified model <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}</annotation></semantics></math> yields <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m3" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]</annotation></semantics></math>, where we denote by <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> the joint marginal of <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> within the original SCM <math id="A2.SS4.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>.</p>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We already recalled in Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Conditional expectation as optimally trained model). ‣ B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> the established fact that fitting a data driven model with standard L2-loss (regression) or cross-entropy-loss (classification) gives an estimate of the conditional expectation. We are thus left with the claim that</p>
<table id="A2.EGx20" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]\,,\\
\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathcal{S}]\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]\,,\\
\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathcal{S}]\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(15)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.2" class="ltx_p">to motivate Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>. The causal graph of <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}</annotation></semantics></math> arises from the one of <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> by deleting all incoming edges to <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math>. In particular, in <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}</annotation></semantics></math> there are no backdoor paths from <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> to <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math>, neither blocked nor unblocked. Hence rule 2 of <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>do</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}</annotation></semantics></math> calculus <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pearl, 2009</a>]</cite> applies and we have</p>
<table id="A2.EGx21" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]\,,\\
\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathcal{S}]\,.\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathcal{S}]\,,\\
\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathcal{S}]\,.\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(16)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS4.p2.3" class="ltx_p">Now, once we intervene on <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> via <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})</annotation></semantics></math> to set it to a fixed value, the actual marginal of <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m10" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> does not influence the distribution of all non-intervened variables <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m11" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{V}\backslash\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒱</mi><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">\</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{V}\backslash\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> so that we can perform the transition <math id="A2.SS4.p2.m12" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">→</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})}\rightarrow\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math> in the following equations</p>
<table id="A2.EGx22" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.E17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.E17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\,,\\
\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\;.\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>X</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>do</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">]</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mrow><msub><mi>𝔼</mi><msup><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo>∼</mo><mi>q</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><mi>do</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>.</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{aligned} \mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}[Y|X_{j},\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\,,\\
\mathbb{E}_{\mathcal{M}^{\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S}\sim q)}}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]&amp;=\mathbb{E}[Y|\mathrm{do}(\mathcal{S})]\;.\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(17)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<div id="A2.SS4.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Combing (<a href="#A2.E17" title="In B.4 Reasoning behind Algorithm ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a>) with (<a href="#A2.E16" title="In B.4 Reasoning behind Algorithm ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>), we obtain the identity (<a href="#A2.E15" title="In B.4 Reasoning behind Algorithm ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a>) that underpins Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<section id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">The exact choice of <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> doesn’t matter.</h4>

<div id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Note, that arguments above never used the fact that <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> is the marginal of <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> within the original model <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{M}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">ℳ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{M}</annotation></semantics></math>. In fact, we can take any distribution <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="q" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>q</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">q</annotation></semantics></math> which is supported on all <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> values which we want to set as context. Another possible choice might be to combine the marginals of <math id="A2.SS4.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="X_{k}\in\mathcal{S}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>∈</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒮</mi></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">X_{k}\in\mathcal{S}</annotation></semantics></math> in an independent manner. For the experiments in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 Experimental results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> we found that such a proceeding gives indeed practically indistinguishable results to the ones presented here.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix C </span>Experimental details and additional results</h2>

<div id="A3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout this work, with the exception of the linear SCM experiment, we used <span id="A3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">XGBoost</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">[<a href="#bib.bib31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chen and Guestrin, 2016</a>]</cite> for fitting data-driven models.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">C.1 </span>Diabetes and breakfast example (Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a>)</h3>

<div id="A3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Example <a href="#S1.Thmtheorem1" title="Example 1.1 (Breakfast and diabetes). ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1.1</span></a> from Section <a href="#S1" title="1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> can be described by an SCM of the following form</p>
<table id="A3.EGx23" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.E18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\begin{aligned} C&amp;=U_{C},\,U_{C}\sim\mathcal{N}(60;25)\,,\\
Y&amp;=U_{Y},\,U_{Y}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.15)\,,\\
G&amp;=85+0.4\cdot C+40\cdot Y+U_{G},U_{G}\sim\mathcal{N}(0;10)\,,\end{aligned}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mtable columnspacing="0pt" rowspacing="0pt"><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>C</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>C</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>60</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>25</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>Y</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo rspace="0.337em">,</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>Y</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.15</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd class="ltx_align_right" columnalign="right"><mi>G</mi></mtd><mtd class="ltx_align_left" columnalign="left"><mrow><mo>=</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>,</mo><mi>U</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo rspace="0.170em" stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>,</mo></mrow></mtd></mtr></mtable><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\begin{aligned} C&amp;=U_{C},\,U_{C}\sim\mathcal{N}(60;25)\,,\\
Y&amp;=U_{Y},\,U_{Y}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.15)\,,\\
G&amp;=85+0.4\cdot C+40\cdot Y+U_{G},U_{G}\sim\mathcal{N}(0;10)\,,\end{aligned}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="1" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equation ltx_align_right">(18)</span></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathrm{Bernoulli}(p)</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the Bernoulli distribution with success probability <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>p</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mathcal{N}(\mu;\sigma^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>μ</mi><mo>,</mo><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mathcal{N}(\mu;\sigma^{2})</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the normal distribution with mean <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\mu" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>μ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\mu</annotation></semantics></math> and variance <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>σ</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma^{2}</annotation></semantics></math>. For computing the Shapley values (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) displayed in Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> (left), we fitted all conditional expectations via XGBoost on <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\cdot 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\cdot 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> random samples from (<a href="#A3.E18" title="In C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>), using Lemma <a href="#A2.Thmtheorem1" title="Lemma B.1 (Conditional expectation as optimally trained model). ‣ B.1 Conditional expectation as optimal model ‣ Appendix B Further theoretical aspects ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B.1</span></a> above. For the cc-Shapley values (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we applied Algorithm <a href="#algorithm1" title="In Using the SCM. ‣ 3.2 Estimation ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>: For each term in (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>), we first modified the SCM accordingly and then used, once more, XGBoost on <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="3\cdot 10^{6}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>6</mn></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">3\cdot 10^{6}</annotation></semantics></math> random samples to fit each conditional expectation. Figure <a href="#S1.F1.sf2" title="In Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1(b)</span></a> was then produced by evaluating (<a href="#S1.E1" title="In 1 Introduction ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) and (<a href="#S3.E6" title="In Definition 3.1. ‣ A causal version of Shapley values. ‣ 3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>) on <math id="A3.SS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="10^{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">10^{4}</annotation></semantics></math> (new) data points drawn from (<a href="#A3.E18" title="In C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">All <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>I</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I</annotation></semantics></math> objects involved in the computation of the Shapley values are plotted in Figure <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Derivation of 𝐼_𝐶⁢(𝐺). ‣ C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>. For cc-Shapley the according objects are plotted in Figure <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Derivation of 𝐼_𝐶⁢(𝐺). ‣ C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>. The univariate terms <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(C)</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.p2.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, shown on the diagonal in Figures <a href="#A3.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ Derivation of 𝐼_𝐶⁢(𝐺). ‣ C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#A3.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ Derivation of 𝐼_𝐶⁢(𝐺). ‣ C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a>, agree for both approaches by definition.</p>
</div>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Derivation of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{C}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{C}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>.</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In Section <a href="#S3.SS1" title="3.1 Using the causal context ‣ 3 Methodology ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3.1</span></a> we gave an explicit expression for <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{C}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{C}(G)</annotation></semantics></math>, which we derive here.
By definition we have, using the independence of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Y" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>Y</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Y</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="C" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>C</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">C</annotation></semantics></math>,</p>
<table id="A3.EGx24" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex9"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I_{C}(G)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{C}(G)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex9.m2" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[Y|G,C]-\mathbb{E}[Y|C]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow><mo>−</mo><mi>𝔼</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo fence="false" rspace="0.167em" stretchy="false">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\mathbb{E}[Y|G,C]-\mathbb{E}[Y|C]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex10"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex10.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(Y=1|G,C)-P(Y=1|C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(Y=1|G,C)-P(Y=1|C)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex11"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex11.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(Y=1|G,C)-P(Y=1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(Y=1|G,C)-P(Y=1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex12"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex12.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=P(Y=1|G,C)-0.15\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=P(Y=1|G,C)-0.15\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">Thus, it remains to estimate <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="P(Y=1|G,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">P(Y=1|G,C)</annotation></semantics></math>.
From the SCM (<a href="#A3.E18" title="In C.1 Diabetes and breakfast example (Example ) ‣ Appendix C Experimental details and additional results ‣ cc-Shapley: Measuring Multivariate Feature Importance Needs Causal Context" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a>) we obtain the joint distribution</p>
<table id="A3.EGx25" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex13"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex13.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(Y,G,C)=\mathrm{Bernoulli}(Y|0.15)\cdot\mathcal{N}(C|60;25)\cdot\mathcal{N}(G|0.4C+85+40Y;10)\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mn>0.15</mn><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mn>60</mn><mo>;</mo><mn>25</mn></mrow><mo rspace="0.055em" stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>85</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mn>40</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>Y</mi></mrow></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(Y,G,C)=\mathrm{Bernoulli}(Y|0.15)\cdot\mathcal{N}(C|60;25)\cdot\mathcal{N}(G|0.4C+85+40Y;10)\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">Writing <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="p=0.15" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">p=0.15</annotation></semantics></math> we can use this expression to compute</p>
<table id="A3.EGx26" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex14"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex14.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle P(Y=1|G,C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle P(Y=1|G,C)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex14.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{P(Y=1,G,C)}{P(Y=1,G,C)+P(Y=0,G,C)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow><mo>,</mo><mi>G</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{P(Y=1,G,C)}{P(Y=1,G,C)+P(Y=0,G,C)}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex15"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex15.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1+\frac{1-p}{p}\frac{\mathcal{N}(G|0.4C+85;10)}{\mathcal{N}(G|0.4C+125;10)}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>85</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>+</mo><mn>125</mn></mrow><mo>;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1+\frac{1-p}{p}\frac{\mathcal{N}(G|0.4C+85;10)}{\mathcal{N}(G|0.4C+125;10)}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex16"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex16.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1+\frac{1-p}{p}e^{-\frac{1}{2\cdot 10^{2}}[(G-0.4C-85)^{2}-(G-0.4C-125)^{2}]}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>85</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>−</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>125</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1+\frac{1-p}{p}e^{-\frac{1}{2\cdot 10^{2}}[(G-0.4C-85)^{2}-(G-0.4C-125)^{2}]}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex17"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex17.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1+e^{-[\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C)-42-\log\frac{1-p}{p}]}}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>+</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mrow><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>42</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>−</mo><mi>p</mi></mrow><mi>p</mi></mfrac></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{1}{1+e^{-[\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C)-42-\log\frac{1-p}{p}]}}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex18"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex18.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle=\sigma\left(\frac{2}{5}\left(G-0.4C-105-\frac{5}{2}\log\frac{17}{3}\right)\right)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>105</mn><mo>−</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>5</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0.167em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo lspace="0.167em">⁡</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>17</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\sigma\left(\frac{2}{5}\left(G-0.4C-105-\frac{5}{2}\log\frac{17}{3}\right)\right)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A3.Ex19"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex19.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\simeq\sigma\left(\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C-109)\right)\,," display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>109</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="0.170em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo>,</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\simeq\sigma\left(\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C-109)\right)\,,</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.4" class="ltx_p">where <math id="A3.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sigma" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>σ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sigma</annotation></semantics></math> denotes the sigmoid function. In total, we thus have</p>
<table id="A3.EGx27" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A3.Ex20"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.Ex20.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle I_{C}(G)\simeq\sigma\left(\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C-109)\right)-0.15\,." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>C</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>≃</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>σ</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo>(</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>−</mo><mrow><mn>0.4</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>109</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>0.15</mn></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0.170em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle I_{C}(G)\simeq\sigma\left(\frac{2}{5}(G-0.4C-109)\right)-0.15\,.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
</div>
<figure id="A3.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<table id="A3.F7.3" class="ltx_tabular ltx_align_middle">
<tr id="A3.F7.3.1" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="A3.F7.3.1.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="A3.F7.3.1.1.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.9pt;">
<span id="A3.F7.3.1.1.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.F7.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{\emptyset}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi mathvariant="normal">∅</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{\emptyset}(C)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td>
<td id="A3.F7.3.1.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_align_top" style="padding-top:1pt;padding-bottom:1pt;">
<span id="A3.F7.3.1.2.1" class="ltx_inline-block ltx_align_top" style="width:113.9pt;">
<span id="A3.F7.3.1.2.1.1" class="ltx_p ltx_align_center"><math id="A3.F7.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="I_{G}(C)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>I</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">I_{G}(C)</annotation></semantics></math></span>
</span></td></tr>
